{
  "title": "React 11: useContext",
  "subtitle": "React Explained",
  "audio": null,
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "problem",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 12.0,
      "text": "The problem: prop drilling.",
      "visual": "flow",
      "badge": "PROP DRILLING",
      "badgeColor": "red",
      "items": [
        [
          "App",
          "yellow",
          "owns the theme"
        ],
        [
          "Layout",
          "muted",
          "just passes it on"
        ],
        [
          "Sidebar",
          "muted",
          "just passes it on"
        ],
        [
          "Button",
          "green",
          "finally uses it"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Sometimes data is needed deep in the tree: a theme, a language, the current user. Passing it as a prop through every layer in between is called prop drilling, and it gets painful fast.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-11-usecontext/problem.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "solution",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 12.0,
      "text": "Context skips the middle.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "const ThemeContext = createContext('light');",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "<ThemeContext value=\"dark\">",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "  <App />",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "</ThemeContext>",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);",
          "green"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Context lets a parent provide a value to its whole subtree. You create a context, wrap part of your tree in its provider with a value, and any descendant can read it with use Context.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-11-usecontext/solution.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "how",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 10.5,
      "text": "How it flows.",
      "visual": "flow",
      "items": [
        [
          "Provider",
          "yellow",
          "sets the value"
        ],
        [
          "Any descendant",
          "blue",
          "reads with useContext"
        ],
        [
          "Value changes",
          "green",
          "all readers re-render"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "The nearest provider above a component wins. When the provided value changes, every component that reads that context re-renders, no matter how deep it is.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-11-usecontext/how.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "perf",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 11.5,
      "text": "Mind the re-renders.",
      "visual": "compare",
      "left": {
        "title": "One big context",
        "color": "red",
        "items": [
          "Any change re-renders",
          "every consumer"
        ]
      },
      "right": {
        "title": "Split contexts",
        "color": "green",
        "items": [
          "Small focused contexts",
          "Memoize the value object"
        ]
      },
      "say": "Because every consumer re-renders when the value changes, don't put everything into one context. Split it into small, focused ones, and memoize the value if it is an object.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-11-usecontext/perf.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "when",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 13.0,
      "text": "When context fits.",
      "visual": "chips",
      "items": [
        [
          "Theme, locale, current user",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "Not for fast-changing state",
          "red"
        ],
        [
          "Not a full state manager",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "Pair it with useReducer",
          "blue"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Context is great for slow-changing, widely needed data, like theme, language, or the current user. It is not built for rapidly changing state, and it is not a complete state management library.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-11-usecontext/when.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 8.5,
      "text": "Wrap it in a custom hook.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "function useTheme() {",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "  const ctx = useContext(ThemeContext);",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "  if (!ctx) {",
          "red"
        ],
        [
          "    throw new Error('no ThemeContext');",
          "red"
        ],
        [
          "  }",
          "red"
        ],
        [
          "  return ctx;",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "}",
          "yellow"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "A good habit: wrap the context in a custom hook, which also throws a helpful error if a component is used outside its provider.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-11-usecontext/hook.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "close",
